fastEmbedR third-party provenance notice

fastEmbedR is being cleaned for permissive distribution. This notice documents
third-party libraries, papers, and implementations reviewed or optionally linked
by the package.

This notice documents code provenance and design references for the cleaned
package. The public API is focused on native UMAP and openTSNE-style embeddings
from data matrices or precomputed KNN matrices. CPU and Metal one-call
workflows use package-native nearest-neighbour implementations. Optional CUDA
builds link directly to the installed RAPIDS cuVS C API. The earlier
legacy t-SNE, InfoTSNE, PaCMAP, TriMap, and LocalMAP reducers were removed from
the exported package API.

Nearest-neighbour ownership and GPU design references
-----------------------------------------------------

fastEmbedR contains a compact CPU HNSW implementation distilled from FAISS
1.14.3 and native Metal exact and IVF-Flat implementations. CPU HNSW retains
FAISS-style hierarchical level sampling, greedy upper-level descent,
ef-construction expansion, diversity pruning, reciprocal links, and parallel
query evaluation. Metal IVF uses a 128-dimensional signed float32 coarse
projection, four native Metal centroid passes, four-SIMD-group fused list
selection, and a two-stage projected-shortlist/exact-rerank search. Four
deterministic pilot strata select `nprobe` and expand the shortlist from 288 to
384 or 512 candidates when needed. Direct Metal reranking is retained as a
safety fallback. Returned neighbours are always ranked with the original
full-dimensional distance.

The Metal list-scan/top-k organization was also informed by the fused
`ivf_list_topk_l2` and batched top-k pattern in MLXPorts/Faiss-mlx. fastEmbedR
implements the design directly in Objective-C++/Metal and does not call Python
or MLX. The FAISS MIT license, Faiss-mlx Apache-2.0 license, exact source
commits, and copyright notices are installed under `LICENSES/`.

The optional companion package faissR continues to provide a broader public
nearest-neighbour API, but fastEmbedR one-call CUDA embeddings do not require
it. `src/native_knn_cuda_impl.cpp` calls installed FAISS GPU `bfKnn` for exact
search and the installed RAPIDS cuVS C API for IVF-Flat. It owns
package-specific device buffers and passes them directly to the embedding
kernels. FAISS/cuVS source and binaries are not vendored in fastEmbedR; their
MIT and Apache-2.0 licenses are retained under `LICENSES/`.

Native Metal PCA design
-----------------------

Metal PCA and openTSNE initialization use a package-native float32
block-subspace TSVD. The heavy forward and transpose matrix products run with
Apple Metal Performance Shaders in a single resident unified-memory workspace.
Only the small projected Gram matrix is handled by fastEmbedR's float32 Jacobi
eigensolver on the CPU. The resident workspace and block-subspace organization
were informed by RAPIDS RAFT TSVD, but no RAPIDS source is copied, vendored, or
called by the Metal implementation. CPU PCA continues to use the
fastPLS-style RSVD family; optional CUDA builds use native RAPIDS RAFT TSVD
when compiled.

The GPU-resident pipeline architecture was informed by:

  mlx-vis
  Author: Han Xiao
  Repository: https://github.com/hanxiao/mlx-vis
  License: Apache-2.0

  annembed
  Author: Jean-Pierre Both
  Repository: https://github.com/jean-pierreBoth/annembed
  License: MIT OR Apache-2.0

The transferred ideas are architectural: retain accelerator buffers across
iterations, organize interpolation-grid scatter/gather as contiguous kernels,
and minimize synchronization between optimizer stages. The implementation is
native R/C++/Metal/CUDA code for fastEmbedR. It does not vendor mlx-vis or
annembed source files, link to those projects, or call Python/MLX at runtime.

The native Metal UMAP backend receives the package's host-prepared CSR fuzzy or
binary graph, packs its active row-neighbour schedule into float32/int32 Metal
buffers once, and keeps layout updates on the GPU for all epochs. Graph
construction time remains labelled separately from Metal optimization. It does
not call Python or MLX.

Native Metal FFT design reference
---------------------------------

The package-native Metal openTSNE FFT-grid path includes a validated 512x512
Stockham FFT kernel implemented using design ideas from:

  AppleSiliconFFT
  Author: Mohamed Amine Bergach
  Repository: https://github.com/aminems/AppleSiliconFFT
  License: MIT

The transferred ideas are radix-4 Stockham autosort stages, threadgroup-memory
staging, and row/column 512-point transform structure. The implementation is
kept inside fastEmbedR's Objective-C++/Metal source, with MIT-compatible
attribution retained here, and is used only for validated 512x512 openTSNE FFT
grids. Other FFT grid sizes use fastEmbedR's generic package-native Metal
Cooley-Tukey kernels.

openTSNE design reference
-------------------------

The native openTSNE-style full t-SNE optimizer and the fixed-reference
transform used by `transform_tsne()` and `landmark_tsne()` were informed by:

  openTSNE
  Repository: https://github.com/pavlin-policar/openTSNE
  License: BSD-3-Clause

For `opentsne_knn()`, `embed_knn(method = "opentsne")`, and `opentsne()`,
fastEmbedR implements a native C++ two-phase optimizer matching openTSNE's
high-level contract: early exaggeration followed by normal optimization,
automatic learning rate `n / exaggeration`, momentum/gain updates,
max-step clipping, sparse positive KNN forces, and native `"fft"` or `"exact"`
negative-gradient choices. The older Barnes-Hut route was removed after the
MNIST 70k benchmarks showed the FFT-grid path was the useful CPU standard.

On macOS with Metal, `opentsne_knn(..., backend = "metal")` receives
host-prepared sparse symmetric affinities and has a native Objective-C++/Metal
exact path for moderate datasets. The exact repulsion, global normalization,
gains/momentum updates, and final layout run in Metal kernels; exact repulsion
is O(n^2) in time. Large-data Metal openTSNE uses a native
FFT/FIt-SNE-style negative-gradient path: interpolation-grid scatter, padded
grid FFT convolution, grid gather, sparse attractive forces, and
gain/momentum updates run in Objective-C++/Metal kernels. CUDA uses package
CUDA kernels plus cuFFT for the corresponding FFT-grid path. The earlier
sampled full-embedding GPU repulsion path is not exposed as openTSNE because it
changes the optimization mathematics.

The implementation is fastEmbedR C++ code and does not vendor, link to, or
call openTSNE's Python or Cython implementation. CPU
`negative_gradient_method = "fft"` is a native grid approximation inspired
by FIt-SNE/openTSNE/t-SNE-CUDA. CPU uses C++ grid FFTs. Metal uses package-native
Objective-C++/Metal grid scatter, FFT convolution, gather, and optimizer
kernels. CUDA uses package-native grid scatter/gather, sparse attractive
forces, gain/momentum kernels, and cuFFT convolution.

t-SNE-CUDA design reference
---------------------------

The native fixed-reference transform design and CPU grid-FFT negative-gradient
path use architectural ideas described in:

  Chan DM, Rao R, Huang F, Canny J. t-SNE-CUDA: GPU-Accelerated t-SNE and its
  Applications to Modern Data. arXiv:1807.11824v1, 2018.

  CannyLab/tsne-cuda
  Repository: https://github.com/CannyLab/tsne-cuda
  License: BSD-3-Clause

The transferred ideas are architectural: keep reference coordinates, query
coordinates, gains, updates, and repulsive-force state resident on the
accelerator during fixed-reference projection; and split t-SNE optimization
into sparse attractive forces plus interpolated/grid negative forces.
fastEmbedR does not vendor, copy, link to, or call t-SNE-CUDA source code.
